==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ang}} ''[[smoc]]''; akin to Old High German ''[[smocho]]'', [[Icelandic]] ''[[smokkr]]'', and from the root of Old English {{term|smgan||to creep}}, akin to [[German]] {{term|schmiegen||to cling to, press close}}. Middle High German ''[[smiegen]]'', Icelandic {{term|smjga||to creep through, to put on a garment which has a hole to put the head through}}; compare with [[Lithuanian]] {{term|smukti||to glide}}. See also ''[[smug]]'', ''[[smuggle]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ɒk}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A woman's [[undergarment]]; a [[shift]]; a [[chemise]].
#: ''In her '''smock''', with head and foot all bare.'' [[w:Chaucer|Chaucer]].
# A [[blouse]].
# A loose [[garment]] worn as [[protection]] by a [[painter]], etc.

